WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.200
Hey, welcome to Fascinating Frontiers, Episode EHT3 from May 28th, 2026.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:07.900
Here's what's happening in space and science today.

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:13.540
A neutrino carrying record energy may have been hurled across the cosmos by a black hole-powered

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:14.060
blazar.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:19.780
The most energetic neutrino, yet detected, could have originated in a blazar powered by

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:21.220
a supermassive black hole.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:27.140
Researchers suggest the particle was accelerated in relativistic jets before traveling across

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:28.460
intergalactic space.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:34.700
Detection relied on observatories that monitor both neutrinos and high energy gamma rays.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:40.820
Confirmation would strengthen the case that blazar's act a efficient cosmic particle accelerators.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:44.860
Additional events will be needed to map the sources with greater precision.

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:51.040
The most energetic neutrino ever recorded may have been launched by a blazar's relativistic jet.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.960
These jets form when material spirals into a supermassive black hole and is ejected at nearly

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:57.820
the speed of light.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:03.040
Detectors on Earth captured the particle's arrival after it crossed billions of light years

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.120
without interacting.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:10.580
Matching its direction with gamma ray observations points to a specific class of active galaxies

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:11.460
as the source.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:17.460
The open question is whether every such high energy neutrino shares the same origin or

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.880
whether multiple acceleration sites contribute.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:25.640
If the sun were scaled to the size of a basketball, the supermassive black hole at a typical

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:30.880
blazar center would be a marble several kilometers across, yet containing the mass of millions

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:32.160
of suns.

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:37.400
Material falling inward forms as whirling disc that generates enormous magnetic fields.

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:43.600
These fields twist and snap, flinging protons and electrons outward in narrow beams that

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.320
trample for billions of light years.

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:51.280
One proton in such a jet can reach energies 100 million times higher than anything produced

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:55.900
in human accelerators, all within a region no larger than our solar system.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:01.700
We can measure the resulting neutrinos and gamma rays when they reach Earth, yet no one

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:06.820
has fully explained why a fraction of particles gain these record energies while most do not.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:12.780
The precise balance between magnetic reconnection, shock waves and plasma instabilities remains

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:13.700
the missing piece.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:19.420
From cosmic particle accelerators to the engineering challenge of building bigger rockets that could

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:22.300
one day carry detectors closer to such sources.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:28.960
Space X is redesigned Starship V3, completed its debut flight last week with the upper-stage

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:29.780
reaching space.

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:34.660
Engineers are now reviewing data from the flight to prepare for the vehicle's return to

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:35.220
flight attempt.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:40.620
The next mission will focus on demonstrating improved engine performance and booster recovery

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:41.160
techniques.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:45.140
Regulators must complete their review before a new launch date is set.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:50.320
Teams at the Texas launch site are already stacking hardware for the follow-on test.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:55.600
While Starship pushes heavy lift boundaries, smaller players are also scaling up through

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:56.740
strategic purchases.

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:02.460
Rocket Lab has purchased and rebranded motive space systems to strengthen its capabilities

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:03.620
in space robotics.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:08.880
The move targets future missions that require precise manipulation on the surface of Mars

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:10.180
and other destinations.

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:15.540
Modus existing hardware and engineering team will integrate into Rocket Lab's growing

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:16.700
spacecraft division.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:22.580
Company leaders expect the combined expertise to support sample handling and instrument deployment

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:23.100
tasks.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:27.420
Development work on new robotic systems is already underway.

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:32.560
Corporate moves like this support the same deep space goals that navigation research aims

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:33.400
to make safer.

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:39.600
A new study demonstrates that pulsars emitting an X-rays could serve as reliable reference

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.400
points for deep space navigation.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:47.940
The timing signals from these rapidly rotating neutron stars remain stable over vast distances.

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:53.940
Researchers simulated spacecraft trajectories using real pulsar data to verify positioning

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:54.540
accuracy.

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:59.900
The approach could reduce reliance on earth-based tracking for missions far from the inner

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:00.720
solar system.

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:04.160
Further hardware tests are planned for future probes.

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:09.760
Better navigation helps future missions reach targets like Mercury, where new ice origin

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:11.180
theories are being tested.

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:17.340
A new study proposes that much of Mercury's water ice arrived in a single slow asteroid

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:19.680
impact within one Mercurian day.

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:25.100
The impactor would have delivered ice that migrated to permanently shadowed craters near

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:25.580
the poles.

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:31.460
Modeling shows the timing and velocity allow ice to survive without immediate sublimation.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:36.880
Earlier theories invoked multiple smaller deliveries over longer periods.

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:42.620
Follow-up observations by upcoming Mercury orbiters could test the single impact scenario.

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:48.360
From Mercury's ancient delivery events to an even older celestial moment that unstopped

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:48.680
a war.

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:57.080
On May 28th, 585 BCE, a total solar eclipse reportedly halted five years of fighting between

00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:58.620
the Lydians and the Meads.

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:04.540
Greek historian Herodotus recorded that the sudden darkness convinced both sides to negotiate

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:06.700
peace and establish a boundary.

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:12.620
The event remains one of the earliest documented cases where an astronomical phenomenon directly

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:14.320
influenced human conflict.

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:19.700
Historians continue to cross-reference ancient records with modern eclipse calculations to

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:20.540
confirm the date.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:26.540
While ancient skis surprise people on the ground, modern skies are about to deliver a second

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:27.860
full moon this month.

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:34.400
A second full moon this month will rise on May 30th, offering observers a narrow window

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:36.480
to catch the moment it clears the horizon.

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:41.980
Viewers must account for local terrain and exact lunar position to avoid missing the

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:42.860
brief alignment.

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:48.320
Photographers are advised to scout locations days ahead to capture the moon appearing

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:50.980
unusually large near foreground objects.

00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:56.100
Atmosphere conditions can shift the apparent color and clarity during the event.

00:05:56.680 --> 00:06:01.040
Clear skies across much of North America should favor early evening attempts.

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:06.120
From backyard observing to the satellites that will soon improve weather forecasting from

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:12.280
orbit, the Mediosat Third Generation Imager 2 satellite has departed France by ship Eiffre

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:13.160
French Guyana.

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:18.560
Once in orbit, the spacecraft will deliver enhanced weather imagery and lightening detection

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:19.840
over Europe and Africa.

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:25.980
The journey marks the final ground transport phase before integration with its Arian 6 launcher.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:30.740
Ground teams will perform final checks upon Aruval at Europe's spaceport.

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:33.120
Launch is scheduled later this year.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:38.400
While one satellite heads to orbit, another heavy lift rocket is preparing for its first

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:39.460
operational mission.

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:46.300
Blue Origin has received FAA clearance to launch its new Glenn rocket carrying 48 Amazon

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:47.580
LEO satellites.

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:52.820
The mission will be the first time the heavy lift vehicle deploys broadband spacecraft

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:54.020
for the constellation.

00:06:54.880 --> 00:07:00.760
The booster, nicknamed No, It's Necessary, will attempt recovery after delivering the payload.

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:05.020
Launch could occur as early as next week pending final range approvals.

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:10.080
Amazon has reserved 24 new Glenn flights for future deployments.

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:13.880
Launch cadence is also tightening on the other side of the country.

00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:19.140
SpaceX and United Launch Alliance are scheduled to conduct back-to-back launches from Florida

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:19.820
facilities.

00:07:20.420 --> 00:07:24.420
The missions will deliver separate payloads into different orbits on the same day.

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:29.100
Weather and range availability will determine the exact order and timing.

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:34.200
Both vehicles are already on their respective paths undergoing final preparations.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:39.300
This marks one of the tighter launch cadences attempted from the state this year.

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:44.200
Keeping orbits usable requires active cleanup efforts already underway.

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:50.260
The UK Space Agency has completed a year of projects focused on cleaning up defunct satellites

00:07:50.260 --> 00:07:56.660
and rocket stages. Multiple missions demonstrated capture and de-orbit techniques for objects in

00:07:56.660 --> 00:08:02.300
low-earth orbit. The effort supports international guidelines aimed at limiting future collision

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:08.280
risks. Data from these demonstrations will inform the design of operational removal services.

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:12.180
Additional funding rounds are expected to expand the program.

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:16.800
Cleaning up near-earth parallels the dust protection work needed on Mars.

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:23.120
Engineering firm StanTech has completed studies on protecting habitats and equipment from fine

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:28.500
Martian dust. The work focuses on sealing systems and filtration methods that must operate

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:33.840
in the planet's low-pressure atmosphere. Prototypes were tested under simulated conditions

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:39.540
matching known dust properties. Results will feed into designs for upcoming landers and surface

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:45.520
infrastructure. NASA and commercial partners are reviewing the recommendations. Habitat protection

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:51.580
research connects directly to long-duration crew health studies. A 10-month stay at an Antarctic

00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:56.660
research station has highlighted psychological and logistical challenges relevant to long-duration

00:08:56.660 --> 00:09:02.120
spaceflight. Participants experience limited communication, confined living quarters,

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:05.880
and delayed resupplies similar to conditions on a Mars mission.

00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:11.620
Researchers recorded sleep patterns, team dynamics, and equipment maintenance issues throughout

00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:16.840
the isolation period. Results are being compared with data from orbital analogs.

00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:21.180
The findings will help refine crew selection criteria and habitat design.

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:26.160
University programs are already supplying talent for the same Mars architecture.

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:31.960
A University of Central Florida graduate now works at NASA on systems supporting future

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:37.900
human missions to Mars. The engineer focuses on habitat and transportation elements within the

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:44.800
broader Artemis to Mars road map. University programs in aerospace engineering supplied foundational

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:50.720
training for the role. On-going collaboration between the school and NASA centers continues to feed

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:56.140
talent into agency projects. Additional graduates are expected to join similar efforts.

00:09:56.920 --> 00:10:01.560
From people to planets, a new technique may soon let us weigh world still hidden in their

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:08.200
birth discs. Astronomers at the University of Warwick, working with MIT and McMaster researchers,

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:13.360
have developed a method to estimate masses of planets still embedded in their birth discs.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:18.560
The technique uses measurable properties of dust rings that form around young stars.

00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:24.520
Published in the astrophysical journal, the approach allows characterization of worlds invisible

00:10:24.520 --> 00:10:29.920
to direct imaging. Observations with current and future telescopes can apply the method across

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:37.000
multiple systems. The team plans to refine the model with additional ALMA data. Space keeps delivering

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:42.040
surprises when we watch closely enough. Next time, we'll be watching for follow-up data on the

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:48.260
starship return to flight attempt and new pulsar navigation hardware tests. That covers today's space

